{
    "title": "Airworthiness Directives; Rolls-Royce Deutschland Ltd & Co KG Engines",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "November 21, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-21/pdf/2025-20598.pdf",
    "summary": "The FAA has issued a new safety directive requiring airlines to inspect and, if needed, replace certain front fan blades on Rolls\u2011Royce Trent 1000 engines after an investigation found some blades may crack due to improper finishing work. The rule takes effect December 8, 2025, and operators will need to complete inspections quickly, roughly within 30 days for most engines, with any cracked parts removed right away.\n\nThis move is meant to prevent a rare but serious risk: a fan blade failure that could send debris out of the engine, force an in\u2011flight shutdown, and endanger the aircraft. Passengers, crews, and the flying public benefit most from the added safety margin. Regulators in the United States and Europe are aligned, which helps ensure a consistent, global response. In the long run, operators also benefit from clearer guidance and reduced risk of unexpected events.\n\nThere are trade\u2011offs. Airlines that use these engines may face short\u2011term schedule disruption while inspections are performed and any affected blades are replaced. The FAA estimates 34 engines in the U.S. are covered. An inspection is relatively modest in cost and time, but a blade replacement is expensive, with parts estimated at about $310,000 per engine, plus labor. Maintenance providers and parts suppliers may feel pressure to meet tight timelines and manage limited component availability.\n\nThe directive follows Europe\u2019s earlier action on the same issue and is being adopted on an accelerated basis because the safety concern is time\u2011sensitive. While the rule is effective immediately, the FAA is still inviting public comments through January 5, 2026. This approach seeks to balance urgency with transparency: act now to reduce risk, then refine as needed with input from the field.",
